# var Point = (function () { function Point(x) { this.x = x; } Point.prototype.get = function () { return this.x; }; return Point; })(): what a transpiler of classes writes.
# The function object is made in a function and not in the body of the module. Link proves that the function runs at most once (one call may enter it, outside of loops, in the body of a module that the loader enters once),
# so the class is evaluated at most once: it has one prototype object. The clock of the order of start follows that one call in place, so the name is laid ahead of every object: no guard of the name, and no read.
# function run(n) { var t = 0; for (var i = 0; i < n; i++) t += new Point(i).get(); return t; }
# The clock of start says of no name that it is laid ahead: through a hole in it the read of such a name gave undefined where the type said a function, and the process died; the claim fired on no real program. What this test told apart by `laid-ahead` it no longer tells.
